{"step_index":23,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:52:47Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-23T22:22:47+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-23T22:22:47+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/find-skill-repo/all%20github%20repos/speech-to-speech/README.md`\nTotal Lines: 629\nTotal Bytes: 26962\nShowing lines 300 to 450\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n300:             \"session\": {\r\n301:                 \"type\": \"realtime\",\r\n302:                 \"instructions\": \"You are a helpful assistant.\",\r\n303:                 \"audio\": {\r\n304:                     \"input\": {\r\n305:                         \"turn_detection\": {\r\n306:                             \"type\": \"server_vad\",\r\n307:                             \"interrupt_response\": True,\r\n308:                         }\r\n309:                     }\r\n310:                 },\r\n311:             },\r\n312:         }\r\n313:     )\r\n314: \r\n315:     for event in conn:\r\n316:         print(event.type)\r\n317: ```\r\n318: \r\n319: The server implements the core Realtime event set: `input_audio_buffer.append`, `session.update`, `conversation.item.create`, `response.create`, and `response.cancel` inbound; speech start/stop, streaming transcription, audio deltas, tool calls, and `response.done` outbound. The full event reference, architecture, and design details live in the [Realtime Engine README](./src/speech_to_speech/api/openai_realtime/README.md).\r\n320: \r\n321: ### LLM Proxy\r\n322: \r\n323: With `--enable_llm_proxy`, the realtime server also exposes the remote LLM it is configured with as a plain OpenAI compatible endpoint, so a client can run side tasks (summaries, titles, background agents) with tools and streaming, fully concurrent with the voice conversation and never interrupted by new speech:\r\n324: \r\n325: * `POST /v1/chat/completions` when running `--llm_backend chat-completions`\r\n326: * `POST /v1/responses` when running `--llm_backend responses-api`\r\n327: \r\n328: The server performs no authentication and no throttling of its own. Enable the proxy only on a trusted network, or deploy the server behind a gateway that owns access control. The s2s-endpoint compute replica is such a gateway: it opens these paths only to clients that created their session with an HF token, checks the API key against that token, and applies a rate limit per user. Point the stock OpenAI SDK at whichever host you talk to; this server ignores the API key (a gateway in front decides what it must be):\r\n329: \r\n330: ```python\r\n331: from openai import OpenAI\r\n332: \r\n333: llm = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8765/v1\", api_key=\"unused\")\r\n334: completion = llm.chat.completions.create(\r\n335:     model=\"anything\",  # ignored: the server forces its configured --model_name\r\n336:     messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarize the conversation so far: ...\"}],\r\n337: )\r\n338: ```\r\n339: \r\n340: Requests are stateless (send the full message list each time) and are proxied to the configured upstream with the key held by the server, which never reaches clients. The `model` field is always overwritten with the server configured `--model_name`. The proxy is off by default, requires a remote backend (`chat-completions` or `responses-api`), and answers 501 with the reason otherwise.\r\n341: \r\n342: ## LLM Backends\r\n343: \r\n344: The LLM is the most compute-intensive and highest-latency component in the pipeline. A single forward pass through a large model can dominate end-to-end response time, so choosing the right backend for your hardware and latency budget matters. The pipeline supports:\r\n345: \r\n346: - **Local inference**: `transformers` on CUDA / CPU and `mlx-lm` on Apple Silicon.\r\n347: - **Self-hosted servers**: `responses-api` and `chat-completions` can point at a local [vLLM](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm) or [llama.cpp](https://github.com/ggerganov/llama.cpp) server.\r\n348: - **Provider APIs**: the same backends work with OpenAI, [HF Inference Providers](https://huggingface.co/inference-providers), [OpenRouter](https://openrouter.ai), and other OpenAI-compatible providers.\r\n349: \r\n350: Two API backends are available, sharing the same `--responses_api_*` connection flags:\r\n351: \r\n352: - `--llm_backend responses-api` (default) targets `/v1/responses`.\r\n353: - `--llm_backend chat-completions` targets `/v1/chat/completions`.\r\n354: \r\n355: The examples below pair Parakeet TDT for local STT and Qwen3-TTS for local TTS with different LLM backends.\r\n356: \r\n357: ### Responses API Backend\r\n358: \r\n359: Works with any provider or server that implements the OpenAI Responses API. Point `--responses_api_base_url` at the endpoint and set `--model_name` accordingly:\r\n360: \r\n361: | Provider / server | `--responses_api_base_url` | `--responses_api_api_key` |\r\n362: |---|---|---|\r\n363: | OpenAI | omit, uses OpenAI default | `$OPENAI_API_KEY` |\r\n364: | HF Inference Providers | `https://router.huggingface.co/v1` | `$HF_TOKEN` |\r\n365: | OpenRouter | `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1` | `$OPENROUTER_API_KEY` |\r\n366: | vLLM | `http://localhost:8000/v1` | omit or any string |\r\n367: | llama.cpp | `http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1` | empty string |\r\n368: \r\n369: ```bash\r\n370: # OpenAI\r\n371: speech-to-speech \\\r\n372:     --mode local \\\r\n373:     --stt parakeet-tdt \\\r\n374:     --llm_backend responses-api \\\r\n375:     --tts qwen3 \\\r\n376:     --qwen3_tts_mlx_quantization 6bit \\\r\n377:     --model_name \"gpt-4o-mini\" \\\r\n378:     --responses_api_api_key \"$OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\r\n379:     --responses_api_stream \\\r\n380:     --enable_live_transcription\r\n381: ```\r\n382: \r\n383: ```bash\r\n384: # HF Inference Providers: Qwen3.5-9B via Together\r\n385: speech-to-speech \\\r\n386:     --mode local \\\r\n387:     --stt parakeet-tdt \\\r\n388:     --llm_backend responses-api \\\r\n389:     --tts qwen3 \\\r\n390:     --qwen3_tts_mlx_quantization 6bit \\\r\n391:     --model_name \"Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B:together\" \\\r\n392:     --responses_api_base_url \"https://router.huggingface.co/v1\" \\\r\n393:     --responses_api_api_key \"$HF_TOKEN\" \\\r\n394:     --responses_api_stream \\\r\n395:     --enable_live_transcription\r\n396: ```\r\n397: \r\n398: ```bash\r\n399: # HF Inference Providers: GPT-oss-20B via Groq\r\n400: speech-to-speech \\\r\n401:     --stt parakeet-tdt \\\r\n402:     --llm_backend responses-api \\\r\n403:     --tts qwen3 \\\r\n404:     --qwen3_tts_mlx_quantization 6bit \\\r\n405:     --model_name \"openai/gpt-oss-20b:groq\" \\\r\n406:     --responses_api_base_url \"https://router.huggingface.co/v1\" \\\r\n407:     --responses_api_api_key \"$HF_TOKEN\" \\\r\n408:     --responses_api_stream \\\r\n409:     --enable_live_transcription\r\n410: ```\r\n411: \r\n412: ### Chat Completions Backend\r\n413: \r\n414: Identical configuration to `responses-api`, reusing the same `--responses_api_*` connection flags, but talks to `/v1/chat/completions` instead of `/v1/responses`. Prefer it when:\r\n415: \r\n416: - the provider ignores `chat_template_kwargs.enable_thinking` on the Responses path and needs a `reasoning_effort` knob to suppress reasoning, or\r\n417: - the server's Responses streaming tool-call path is unreliable, while its Chat Completions tool-call streaming is solid. This is useful for some vLLM builds; see [#312](https://github.com/huggingface/speech-to-speech/issues/312).\r\n418: \r\n419: Add `--responses_api_reasoning_effort none` to disable reasoning on providers where the chat-template flag has no effect:\r\n420: \r\n421: ```bash\r\n422: # vLLM serving a Qwen model with tool calling\r\n423: speech-to-speech \\\r\n424:     --mode realtime \\\r\n425:     --stt parakeet-tdt \\\r\n426:     --llm_backend chat-completions \\\r\n427:     --tts qwen3 \\\r\n428:     --model_name \"Qwen/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507\" \\\r\n429:     --responses_api_base_url \"http://localhost:8000/v1\" \\\r\n430:     --responses_api_stream\r\n431: ```\r\n432: \r\n433: ```bash\r\n434: # Gemma 4 31B via the HF router on Cerebras, with reasoning disabled for low voice latency\r\n435: speech-to-speech \\\r\n436:     --mode realtime \\\r\n437:     --stt parakeet-tdt \\\r\n438:     --llm_backend chat-completions \\\r\n439:     --tts qwen3 \\\r\n440:     --model_name \"google/gemma-4-31B-it:cerebras\" \\\r\n441:     --responses_api_base_url \"https://router.huggingface.co/v1\" \\\r\n442:     --responses_api_api_key \"$HF_TOKEN\" \\\r\n443:     --responses_api_reasoning_effort none \\\r\n444:     --responses_api_stream\r\n445: ```\r\n446: \r\n447: ### Fully Local\r\n448: \r\n449: Run the LLM in a separate llama.cpp process for the lowest-friction fully local setup, as shown in the [Reachy Mini local conversation guide](https://huggingface.co/blog/local-reachy-mini-conversation):\r\n450: \r\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":24,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:52:47Z","thinking":"**Analyzing Speech Systems**\n\nI'm currently focused on a low-latency voice agent pipeline. The modular design, built around VAD, STT, LLM, and TTS components, allows for interchangeable backends such as Silero VAD and Parakeet/Whisper STT. The pipeline is designed for real-time interaction using a WebSocket/WebRTC API compatible with OpenAI's format.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\Downloads\\find-skill-repo\\all github repos\\OmniVoice\\README.md","EndLine":150,"StartLine":1,"toolAction":"Viewing OmniVoice README","toolSummary":"Read OmniVoice README"}}]}
{"step_index":25,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:52:52Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-23T22:22:52+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-23T22:22:52+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/find-skill-repo/all%20github%20repos/OmniVoice/README.md`\nTotal Lines: 424\nTotal Bytes: 17252\nShowing lines 1 to 150\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # OmniVoice 🌍\r\n2: \r\n3: <p align=\"center\">\r\n4:   <img width=\"200\" height=\"200\" alt=\"OmniVoice\" src=\"https://zhu-han.github.io/omnivoice/pics/omnivoice.jpg\" />\r\n5: </p>\r\n6: \r\n7: <p align=\"center\">\r\n8:   <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/k2-fsa/OmniVoice\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/%F0%9F%A4%97%20Hugging%20Face-Model-FFD21E\" alt=\"Hugging Face Model\"></a>\r\n9:   &nbsp;\r\n10:   <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/spaces/k2-fsa/OmniVoice\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/%F0%9F%A4%97%20Hugging%20Face-Space-blue\" alt=\"Hugging Face Space\"></a>\r\n11:   &nbsp;\r\n12:   <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.00688\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/arXiv-Paper-B31B1B.svg\"></a>\r\n13:   &nbsp;\r\n14:   <a href=\"https://zhu-han.github.io/omnivoice\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/GitHub.io-Demo_Page-blue?logo=GitHub&style=flat-square\"></a>\r\n15:   &nbsp;\r\n16:   <a href=\"https://colab.research.google.com/github/k2-fsa/OmniVoice/blob/master/docs/OmniVoice.ipynb\"><img src=\"https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg\" alt=\"Open In Colab\"></a>\r\n17: </p>\r\n18: \r\n19: OmniVoice is a state-of-the-art massively multilingual zero-shot text-to-speech (TTS) model supporting over 600 languages. Built on a novel diffusion language model-style architecture, it generates high-quality speech with superior inference speed, supporting voice cloning and voice design.\r\n20: \r\n21: **Contents**: [Key Features](#key-features) | [Installation](#installation) | [Quick Start](#quick-start) | [Python API](#python-api) | [Command-Line Tools](#command-line-tools) | [Training & Evaluation](#training--evaluation) | [Discussion](#discussion--communication) | [Citation](#citation)\r\n22: \r\n23: ## Key Features\r\n24: \r\n25: - **600+ Languages Supported**: The broadest language coverage among zero-shot TTS models ([full list](docs/languages.md)).\r\n26: - **Voice Cloning**: State-of-the-art voice cloning quality.\r\n27: - **Voice Design**: Control voices via assigned speaker attributes (gender, age, pitch, dialect/accent, whisper, etc.).\r\n28: - **Fine-grained Control**: Non-verbal symbols (e.g., `[laughter]`) and pronunciation correction via pinyin or phonemes.\r\n29: - **Fast Inference**: RTF as low as 0.025 (40x faster than real-time).\r\n30: - **Diffusion Language Model-style Architecture**: A clean, streamlined, and scalable design that delivers both quality and speed.\r\n31: \r\n32: ---\r\n33: \r\n34: ## Installation\r\n35: \r\n36: Choose **one** of the following methods: **pip** or **uv**.\r\n37: \r\n38: ### pip\r\n39: \r\n40: > We recommend using a fresh virtual environment (e.g., `conda`, `venv`, etc.) to avoid conflicts.\r\n41: \r\n42: **Step 1**: Install PyTorch\r\n43: \r\n44: <details>\r\n45: <summary>NVIDIA GPU</summary>\r\n46: \r\n47: ```bash\r\n48: # Install pytorch with your CUDA version, e.g.\r\n49: pip install torch==2.8.0+cu128 torchaudio==2.8.0+cu128 --extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128\r\n50: ```\r\n51: > See [PyTorch official site](https://pytorch.org/get-started/locally/) for other versions installation.\r\n52: \r\n53: </details>\r\n54: \r\n55: <details>\r\n56: <summary>Apple Silicon</summary>\r\n57: \r\n58: ```bash\r\n59: pip install torch==2.8.0 torchaudio==2.8.0\r\n60: ```\r\n61: \r\n62: </details>\r\n63: \r\n64: <details>\r\n65: <summary>Intel Arc GPU (XPU)</summary>\r\n66: \r\n67: Intel Arc GPUs (Alchemist and Battlemage architectures) are supported via PyTorch's XPU backend.\r\n68: \r\n69: 1. Install the [Intel GPU drivers](https://dgpu-docs.intel.com/driver/installation.html) for your OS.\r\n70: \r\n71: 2. Install PyTorch with XPU support from Intel's wheel index:\r\n72: \r\n73: ```bash\r\n74: pip install torch torchaudio --index-url https://pytorch-extension.intel.com/release-whl/stable/xpu/us/\r\n75: ```\r\n76: \r\n77: > See [Intel's PyTorch XPU guide](https://intel.github.io/intel-extension-for-pytorch/xpu/latest/) for version-specific instructions.\r\n78: \r\n79: 3. Verify the backend is working:\r\n80: \r\n81: ```bash\r\n82: python -c \"import torch; print(torch.xpu.is_available(), torch.xpu.device_count())\"\r\n83: ```\r\n84: \r\n85: **Notes**:\r\n86: - `flash_attn` is not available on XPU; the model automatically falls back to SDPA.\r\n87: - Training with packed sequences (`flex_attention`) has partial XPU support; single-GPU SDPA training should work.\r\n88: - Tested on Arc A310 (Alchemist, 4 GB) and Arc Pro B50 (Battlemage, 16 GB).\r\n89: \r\n90: </details>\r\n91: \r\n92: **Step 2**: Install OmniVoice (choose one)\r\n93: \r\n94: ```bash\r\n95: # From PyPI (stable release)\r\n96: pip install omnivoice\r\n97: \r\n98: # From the latest source on GitHub (no need to clone)\r\n99: pip install git+https://github.com/k2-fsa/OmniVoice.git\r\n100: \r\n101: # For development (clone first, editable install)\r\n102: git clone https://github.com/k2-fsa/OmniVoice.git\r\n103: cd OmniVoice\r\n104: pip install -e .\r\n105: ```\r\n106: \r\n107: ### uv\r\n108: \r\n109: Clone the repository and sync dependencies:\r\n110: \r\n111: ```bash\r\n112: git clone https://github.com/k2-fsa/OmniVoice.git\r\n113: cd OmniVoice\r\n114: uv sync\r\n115: ```\r\n116: \r\n117: > **Tip**: Can use mirror with `uv sync --default-index \"https://mirrors.aliyun.com/pypi/simple\"`\r\n118: \r\n119: ---\r\n120: \r\n121: ## Quick Start\r\n122: \r\n123: Try OmniVoice without coding:\r\n124: \r\n125: - Launch the local web UI: `omnivoice-demo --ip 0.0.0.0 --port 8001`\r\n126: \r\n127: - Or try it directly on [HuggingFace Space](https://huggingface.co/spaces/k2-fsa/OmniVoice)\r\n128: \r\n129: - Or run it in Google Colab: [![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/k2-fsa/OmniVoice/blob/master/docs/OmniVoice.ipynb)\r\n130: \r\n131: > If you have trouble connecting to HuggingFace when downloading the pre-trained models, set `export HF_ENDPOINT=\"https://hf-mirror.com\"` before running.\r\n132: \r\n133: For full usage, see the [Python API](#python-api) and [Command-Line Tools](#command-line-tools) sections below.\r\n134: \r\n135: ---\r\n136: \r\n137: ## Python API\r\n138: \r\n139: OmniVoice supports three generation modes. All features in this section are also available via [command-line tools](#command-line-tools).\r\n140: \r\n141: ### Voice Cloning\r\n142: \r\n143: Clone a voice from a short reference audio. Provide `ref_audio` and `ref_text`:\r\n144: \r\n145: ```python\r\n146: from omnivoice import OmniVoice\r\n147: import soundfile as sf\r\n148: import torch\r\n149: \r\n150: model = OmniVoice.from_pretrained(\r\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
