{"step_index":27,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:52:55Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-23T22:22:55+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-23T22:22:55+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/find-skill-repo/all%20github%20repos/OmniVoice/README.md`\nTotal Lines: 424\nTotal Bytes: 17252\nShowing lines 150 to 300\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n150: model = OmniVoice.from_pretrained(\r\n151:     \"k2-fsa/OmniVoice\",\r\n152:     device_map=\"cuda:0\",\r\n153:     dtype=torch.float16\r\n154: )\r\n155: # Apple Silicon users: use device_map=\"mps\" instead\r\n156: # Intel Arc GPU users: use device_map=\"xpu\" instead\r\n157: \r\n158: audio = model.generate(\r\n159:     text=\"Hello, this is a test of zero-shot voice cloning.\",\r\n160:     ref_audio=\"ref.wav\",\r\n161:     ref_text=\"Transcription of the reference audio.\",\r\n162: ) # audio is a list of `np.ndarray` with shape (T,) at 24 kHz.\r\n163: \r\n164: # If you don't want to input `ref_text` manually, you can directly omit the `ref_text`.\r\n165: # The model will use Whisper ASR to auto-transcribe it. To use a local copy (or\r\n166: # a different Whisper model), pass `asr_model_name=\"...\"` to `from_pretrained`.\r\n167: # To control which device Whisper is loaded on (e.g. another GPU in multi-GPU\r\n168: # setups, or the CPU), pass `asr_device=\"cuda:1\"` (or `\"cpu\"`).\r\n169: \r\n170: sf.write(\"out.wav\", audio[0], 24000)\r\n171: ```\r\n172: \r\n173: #### Reusing a cloned voice across sessions\r\n174: \r\n175: Encode the reference audio once, save the resulting prompt, and skip the\r\n176: audio loading / auto-transcription steps in later sessions:\r\n177: \r\n178: ```python\r\n179: prompt = model.create_voice_clone_prompt(\r\n180:     ref_audio=\"ref.wav\", ref_text=\"Transcription of the reference audio.\"\r\n181: )\r\n182: prompt.save(\"my_voice.pt\")\r\n183: \r\n184: # Later, in a new session:\r\n185: from omnivoice import VoiceClonePrompt\r\n186: \r\n187: prompt = VoiceClonePrompt.load(\"my_voice.pt\")\r\n188: audio = model.generate(text=\"Hello again!\", voice_clone_prompt=prompt)\r\n189: ```\r\n190: \r\n191: > **Tips**\r\n192: >\r\n193: > - Use a 3–10 seconds reference audio clip. Longer audio slows down inference and may degrade cloning quality.\r\n194: > - For standard pronunciation, use a reference audio in the **same language** as the target speech. In cross-lingual voice cloning (i.e., the reference audio and target speech are in different languages), the generated speech will carry an accent from the reference audio's language.\r\n195: > - For better results with Arabic numerals, normalize them to words first (e.g., \"123\" → \"one hundred twenty-three\"). You can pass `normalize_text=True` to `generate()` to do this automatically (opt-in; install the extra with `pip install \"omnivoice[tn]\"`, which pulls in [WeTextProcessing](https://github.com/wenet-e2e/WeTextProcessing)):\r\n196: >\r\n197: >   ```python\r\n198: >   # \"I have 2345 apples.\" is read correctly instead of digit-by-digit.\r\n199: >   audio = model.generate(text=\"I have 2345 apples.\", normalize_text=True)\r\n200: >   ```\r\n201: >\r\n202: >   Chinese and English use WeTextProcessing; other languages fall back to `num2words` for integers. Inline control syntax (`[laughter]`, `[B EY1 S]`, pinyin tone markers) is preserved. On macOS (Apple Silicon), `pynini` has no wheel — install it via `conda install -c conda-forge pynini` first.\r\n203: >\r\n204: > For more tips, see [docs/tips.md](docs/tips.md).\r\n205: \r\n206: ### Voice Design\r\n207: \r\n208: Describe the desired voice with speaker attributes — no reference audio needed.\r\n209: Supported attributes: **gender** (male/female), **age** (child to elderly),\r\n210: **pitch** (very low to very high), **style** (whisper), **English accent**\r\n211: (American, British, etc.), and **Chinese dialect** (四川话, 陕西话, etc.).\r\n212: Attributes are comma-separated and freely combinable across categories.\r\n213: \r\n214: ```python\r\n215: audio = model.generate(\r\n216:     text=\"Hello, this is a test of zero-shot voice design.\",\r\n217:     instruct=\"female, low pitch, british accent\",\r\n218: )\r\n219: ```\r\n220: \r\n221: > **Note**: The model is primarily trained on the voice cloning task, so voice cloning is the most stable mode. Voice design is trained on Chinese and English data only. It can generalize to other languages, but may produce unstable results for some low-resource languages or edge cases.\r\n222: \r\n223: See [docs/voice-design.md](docs/voice-design.md) for the full attribute\r\n224: reference, Chinese equivalents, and usage tips.\r\n225: \r\n226: ### Auto Voice\r\n227: \r\n228: Let the model choose a voice automatically:\r\n229: \r\n230: ```python\r\n231: audio = model.generate(text=\"This is a sentence without any voice prompt.\")\r\n232: ```\r\n233: \r\n234: ### Generation Parameters\r\n235: \r\n236: All above three modes share the same `model.generate()` API. You can further control the generation behavior via keyword arguments:\r\n237: \r\n238: ```python\r\n239: audio = model.generate(\r\n240:     text=\"...\",\r\n241:     num_step=32,  # diffusion steps (or 16 for faster inference)\r\n242:     speed=1.0,     # speed factor (>1.0 faster, <1.0 slower)\r\n243:     duration=10.0, # fixed output duration in seconds (overrides speed)\r\n244:     # ... more options\r\n245: )\r\n246: ```\r\n247: See more detailed control in [docs/generation-parameters.md](docs/generation-parameters.md).\r\n248: \r\n249: ### Non-Verbal & Pronunciation Control\r\n250: \r\n251: OmniVoice supports inline **non-verbal symbols** and **pronunciation correction** within the input text.\r\n252: \r\n253: **Non-verbal symbols**: Insert tags like `[laughter]` directly in the text to add expressive non-verbal sounds.\r\n254: \r\n255: ```python\r\n256: audio = model.generate(text=\"[laughter] You really got me. I didn't see that coming at all.\")\r\n257: ```\r\n258: \r\n259: Supported tags: `[laughter]`, `[sigh]`, `[confirmation-en]`, `[question-en]`, `[question-ah]`, `[question-oh]`, `[question-ei]`, `[question-yi]`, `[surprise-ah]`, `[surprise-oh]`, `[surprise-wa]`, `[surprise-yo]`, `[dissatisfaction-hnn]`.\r\n260: \r\n261: **Pronunciation control (Chinese)**: Use pinyin with tone numbers to correct specific character pronunciations.\r\n262: \r\n263: ```python\r\n264: audio = model.generate(text=\"这批货物打ZHE2出售后他严重SHE2本了，再也经不起ZHE1腾了。\")\r\n265: ```\r\n266: \r\n267: **Pronunciation control (English)**: Use [CMU pronunciation dictionary](https://svn.code.sf.net/p/cmusphinx/code/trunk/cmudict/cmudict.0.7a)  (uppercase, in brackets) to override default English pronunciations.\r\n268: \r\n269: ```python\r\n270: audio = model.generate(text=\"He plays the [B EY1 S] guitar while catching a [B AE1 S] fish.\")\r\n271: ```\r\n272: \r\n273: ---\r\n274: \r\n275: ## Command-Line Tools\r\n276: \r\n277: Three CLI entry points are provided. The CLI tools support all features available in the Python API (voice cloning, voice design, auto voice, generation parameters, etc.) — all controlled via command-line arguments.\r\n278: \r\n279: | Command | Description | Source |\r\n280: |---|---|---|\r\n281: | `omnivoice-demo` | Interactive Gradio web demo | [omnivoice/cli/demo.py](omnivoice/cli/demo.py) |\r\n282: | `omnivoice-infer` | Single-item inference | [omnivoice/cli/infer.py](omnivoice/cli/infer.py) |\r\n283: | `omnivoice-infer-batch` | Batch inference across multiple GPUs | [omnivoice/cli/infer_batch.py](omnivoice/cli/infer_batch.py) |\r\n284: \r\n285: ### Demo\r\n286: \r\n287: ```bash\r\n288: omnivoice-demo --ip 0.0.0.0 --port 8001\r\n289: ```\r\n290: \r\n291: Provides a web UI for voice cloning and voice design. See `omnivoice-demo --help` for all options.\r\n292: \r\n293: ### Single Inference\r\n294: \r\n295: ```bash\r\n296: # Voice Cloning\r\n297: # ref_text can be omitted (Whisper will auto-transcribe ref_audio to get it).\r\n298: omnivoice-infer \\\r\n299:     --model k2-fsa/OmniVoice \\\r\n300:     --text \"This is a test for text to speech.\" \\\r\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
